CONTEXT. This page continues Gauss's search (Litt. G) for a lattice-point proof of the biquadratic reciprocity law in Z[i]. m and M are Gaussian integers (M = 1+2i is the worked example). The previous page ends: 'A fruitful idea seems to be to examine how the relations change when m increases by 2M.' This page sets up the machinery for that and starts the computation.

(A) DEFINITION. For complex t = x+iy and t' = x'+iy', S(t,t') is the signed number of lattice points in the trapezoid bounded by the real axis, the ordinates (vertical lines) through t and t', and the segment tt'. Gauss adds 'with suitable restrictions', meaning boundary conventions. My reading is S(t,t') = sum over integers u with x < u <= x' of floor(height of segment tt' at u), with the sign reversed when x' < x.

(B) TRANSFORMATION RULES. [x] denotes the floor.
(1) S(it,it') = S(t,t') + [x][y] - [x'][y'].
(2) S(-it,-it') = S(t,t') + [x+1][y] - [x'+1][y'].
(3) S(-t,-t') = S(t,t') - [x] + [x'].
(4) S(t+g+hi, t'+g+hi) = S(t,t') + h[x'] - h[x] for rational integers g, h.
Reasons (mine):
- (1) Let A be the points under the segment in the vertical strip and B the points left of the segment in the horizontal strip. For a monotone segment in the first quadrant, A + B = [x'][y'] - [x][y]. Multiplication by i turns the vertical-strip count of it,it' into -B, giving (1).
- (2) and (3) are the analogous statements for the rotations by -90 and 180 degrees. The shifted floors reflect which boundary points are counted.
- (4) A vertical shift by h adds h points above each integer abscissa in the strip. A horizontal integer shift changes nothing.

(C) POLYGON FORMULA. For a rectilinear figure whose boundary is run through t, t', t'', ... in the sense drawn, the number of interior points is S(t,t',t'',...) = S(t,t') + S(t',t'') + S(t'',t''') + ... (the trapezoid decomposition). Consequence (mine): for a closed polygon the correction terms in (1)-(4) telescope away. So S(iP) = S(P), and S(P+g+hi) = S(P) for Gaussian-integer translations.

(D) THE FIGURE, M = 1+2i. Put w = m/(2M); the numbered points are:
- 0;
- 1 = iw;
- 2 = (-1+i)w;
- 3 = (-1+2i)w;
- 4 = 2iw;
- 5 = m/4;
- 6 = (1/2+i/4)m = (5/2)i w;
- 7 = (1/4+i/2)m = (-3/2+2i)w;
- 8 = im/4;
- 9 = m/2 = (1+2i)w = Mw;
- 10 = (1+i)m/2 = (-1+3i)w;
- 11 = im/2 = (-2+i)w.
So the half-square 0, 9, 10, 11 (side m/2) is overlaid by the grid generated by w and iw, together with its quarter points 5, 6, 7, 8. The 'deciding' count (Decident), which determines the biquadratic character in a Gauss-lemma-type criterion, is a signed sum of 16 edge terms:
- negative: S(0,1), S(10,3), S(4,6), S(4,9), S(4,3), S(4,1), S(2,1), S(2,3), S(2,11), S(2,8), S(5,1), S(7,3);
- positive: S(0,8), S(5,9), S(10,6), S(7,11).
The deleted entries are +S(9,4) and +S(10,11).

(E) THE OPERATOR Delta. Delta is the change of a quantity when m -> m+2M. Every labelled point then moves by a Gaussian integer: w -> w+1, so point 1 moves by i, point 4 by 2i, point 9 by M, point 6 by 5i/2, and so on.

Delta S(0,1) = S(0,(1)+i) - S(0,(1)). By (C), with the vertical side S((1),(1)+i) = 0, this is -S(0,(1),(1)+i). Rotating by -i, which is free for a closed polygon, gives -S(0, w, w+1).

The other five terms of the first group are treated the same way. Each becomes 'boundary terms' plus minus a copy of the small triangle (0, w, w+1), rotated and translated:
- Delta S(4,1) = 2R(1) - 2R(4) - S(-2w, -w, -w+1);
- Delta S(4,9) = 2R9 + 2 - 2R4 + J9 - S(2iw, 2iw+w, 2iw+w+1);
- Delta S(4,3) = 2R3 - 2 - 2R4 - J3 - S(-2iw, -2iw+w, -2iw+w+1);
- Delta S(4,6) = 2R6 - 2R4 - S(2w, 2w+w/2, 2w+w/2+1/2), a half-scale triangle because the edge 4-6 is half a grid step;
- Delta S(7,3) = 2R3 - 2R(7+1/2) + J3 - J7(R(7+1/2) - R7) - S(a 3/4-size piece).
R(.) is evidently the floor of a real part and J an integer or floor-type correction of the imaginary part (my reading; not defined on the page).

The sum of the six is 2R1 + 4R3 + 2R6 + 2R9 - 2R(7+1/2) - 8R4 + J9 - J7(R(7+1/2) - R7) - S(0, m/2, m/2+M). The six small pieces fit together into the triangle (0, Mw, M(w+1)), which is the small triangle scaled by M, of norm 5. Margin note: S(0, 9, 9+M).

The page then begins the second group:
- Delta S(10,3) = 3R(3) - 3R(10) - S((1/2+i/2)m/M, ...);
- Delta S(2,3) = R(3) - R(2) - S(1 + i·m/(2M), ...).
The following page completes this group to -S(1/2+i/2, ..., ...), adds all 12, and treats the 4 positive edges.
